      {
        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 5,
        "titulo": "Greeting people: which greeting to use",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: «bonjour» (bohn-ZHOOR) works in the morning and during the day. It is polite with everybody: friends, neighbors, shopkeepers. STEP 2: «bonsoir» (bohn-SWAHR) works from late afternoon onward. You only say it when you arrive. STEP 3: «salut» (sa-LEW) is informal. It works for «hi» and also for «bye», but only with friends and family. STEP 4: «au revoir» (oh ruh-VWAHR) is the polite «goodbye» for anyone. STEP 5: to ask how someone is, say «Ça va ?» (sa va). The most common answer is «Ça va bien, merci.» (sa va byan, mehr-SEE). And to return the question, say «Et toi ?» (ay TWAH). STEP 6: when you walk into a shop in France, always say «bonjour». When you leave, say «au revoir» or «merci, au revoir».",
        "comparacao": "In English, «hi» and «bye» are casual too. In French, «salut» does both jobs, but «bonjour» and «au revoir» are more polite. Saying «bonjour» when you walk into a shop is practically mandatory in French culture.",
        "exemplos": [
          {
            "ru": "Bonjour, madame !",
            "pt": "Good morning, madam!"
          },
          {
            "ru": "Bonsoir, monsieur !",
            "pt": "Good evening, sir!"
          },
          {
            "ru": "Salut ! Ça va ?",
            "pt": "Hi! How are you?"
          },
          {
            "ru": "Ça va bien, merci. Et toi ?",
            "pt": "Fine, thank you. And you?"
          },
          {
            "ru": "Merci, au revoir !",
            "pt": "Thank you, bye!"
          }
        ],
        "erros": [
